<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-calculus">
  <title>Newton, Leibniz, and the Calculus (8.2-8.4)</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Forerunners-of-Calculus">
    <title>Forerunners of Calculus</title>

    <p>
      Calculus emerged in the 17th century with the discovery that two of the most well known problems of mathematics at the time, the area problem and the tangent line problem are essentially inverses of each other. During the 16th and 17th centuries many mathematicians worked on one or both of these problems and some developed techniques for solving them, but when it comes to the discovery of calculus, there are three things that are required for a theory of calculus:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            A systemic method for finding areas.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A systemic method for finding tangents.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A profound connection between the area and tangent problems.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz independently came up with methods that satisfied all three requirements and thus they are considered the discoverers of calculus; however, they did not develop their ideas out of nothing, but were influenced by the mathematicians who had come before them. To better appreciate the magnitude of their discoveries, it is helpful to consider the state of mathematics in the 17th century before the discovery of calculus.
    </p>

    <p>
      The problem of finding the area of geometric shapes goes back to ancient times; however, in the 17th century new techniques were developed to calculate areas. A common technique was the <term>method of indivisibles</term> which was popularized by Bonaventura Cavalieri and is also known as <term>Cavalieri's principle</term>. In the two dimensional case the method of indivisibles states that if two regions of a plane are included between two parallel lines in that plane and if every line parallel to those lines intersects both regions in line segments of equal length than the two regions have equal areas. Cavalieri considered a 2D object such as a square to be made up of an infinite number of lines. If those lines were shifted up or down, but their lengths stayed the same then the resulting shape would have the same area as the square. The method of indivisibles can also be extended to volumes in which the lines between regions are replaced by planes whose cross sections have equal areas. Using these techniques Cavalieri was able to determine areas and volumes of many shapes.
    </p>

    <p>
      The method of indivisibles has some noticeable drawbacks. First off it is not entirely rigorous since it implies that a 2D shape is made up of an infinite number of lines with no area. This makes it very easy to obtain incorrect results. For these reasons the method of indivisibles was replaced with the <term>method of infinitessimals</term>. Whereas the indivisibles had dimension one less than the figure, infinitessimals were of the same dimension as the figure. For example instead of a square being made up as an infinite number of lines, in the method of infinitessimals it would be thought of as made up of infinitely many rectangles of infinitessimal width. While more rigorous than the method of indivisibles, the method of infinitessimals was not without flaws. In particular if a square is made of infinitely many rectangles and those rectangles all have positive area (no matter how small) then it would seem that the square must have infinite area. Mathematicians justified this by saying that the rectangles had width that was smaller than any positive quantity, but not zero. 
    </p>

    <p>
      A very different approach to the area problem was developed by the English mathematician John Wallis by 1676. Unlike the other approaches to the area problem, this one uses an arithmetic approach rather than a geometric one. We can illustrate Wallis' method by considering the area beneath the parabola <m>y = x^2</m> on the interval <m>[0,1]</m>. Wallis' approach is based off the following observations:
      <md>
        <mrow> \frac{0^2+1^2}{1^2+1^2} \amp = \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{6} </mrow>
        <mrow> \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2}{2^2+2^2+2^2} \amp = \frac{5}{12} = \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{12} </mrow>
         <mrow> \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2+3^2}{3^2+3^2+3^2+3^2} \amp = \frac{7}{18} = \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{18} </mrow>
         <mrow> \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2+3^2+4^2}{4^2+4^2+4^2+4^2+4^2} \amp = \frac{3}{8} = \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{24} </mrow>
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Wallis concluded that in general
      <md>
        \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2+3^2+ \ldots + n^2}{n^2+n^2+n^2+n^2+ \ldots + n^2} = \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{6n}
      </md>
      By taking <m>n</m> to have larger and larger values, the right hand side approaches the limiting value of <m>\frac{1}{3}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      To determine the area under the parabola <m>y= x^2</m> from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=a</m>, Wallis considered the area under the parabola to be made up of <m>n</m> infinitely narrow vertical rectangles each with width <m>\frac{a}{n}</m>. The ratio of the sum of the areas of these rectangles to the rectangle cirumscribing the entire parabola is
      <md>
        \frac{(\frac{0a}{n})^2+(\frac{1a}{n})^2+(\frac{2a}{n})^2+ \ldots + (\frac{0a}{n})^2}{a^2+a^2+a^2+ \ldots + a^2} = \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2+\ldots + n^2}{n^2+n^2 +\ldots + n^2}
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Wallis thus concluded that the ratio of the area underneath the parabola to the area of the circumscribing rectangle was
      <md>
        \lim_{n\to \infty}  \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2+\ldots + n^2}{n^2+n^2 +\ldots + n^2} = \frac{1}{3}.
      </md>
      In modern notation this says that
      <md>
        \frac{\int_0^a x^2dx}{a^3} = \frac{1}{3}
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      After working out several special cases, Wallis concluded that 
      <md>
        \int_0^a x^k dx = \frac{a^{k+1}}{k+1}
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      The other important problem in 17th century European mathematics was finding tangent lines. Pierre de Fermat gave a formula for finding tangent lines that was based off his <term>adequality</term> method for finding the maximum or minimum of functions. As an illustration we demonstrate how this method is used to maximize a quantity. Suppose we want to divide a line segment <m>AC</m> into 2 pieces at a point <m>E</m> so that the product <m>|AE| \times |EC|</m> is maximized. Fermat described his solution of the problem as follows.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        We write <m>AC = b</m>; let <m>a</m> be one of the segments so that the other will be <m>b-a</m>, and the product, the maximum of which is to be found will be <m>ba-a^2</m>. Let now <m>a+e</m> be the first segment of <m>b</m>; the second will be <m>b-a-e</m>, and the product of the segments, <m>ba-a^2 +be -2ae-e^2</m>; this must be adequated with the preceding: <m>ba-a^2</m>. Suppressing common terms <m>be \sim 2ae + e^2</m>. Suppressing <m>e: b = 2a</m>. To solve the problem we must consequently take the half of <m>b</m>. 
      </p>

      <p>
        We can hardly expect a more general method.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Fermat's method is based off an assumption that the value of an expression doesn't change much if you are close to a maximum or minimum. Thus if the maximum occurs at <m>a</m> then <m>(b-a)\times a</m> must be close to <m> (b-(a-e)) \times (a+e)</m>. Expanding this out, this implies that <m>be</m> must be approximately equal to <m>2ae+e^2</m>. Dividing both sides by <m>e</m> gives that <m>b</m> is approximately <m>2a+e</m>. Since <m>e</m> needs to be sufficiently small for this to be true, we can take <m>e=0</m> and conclude that <m>b = 2a</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      As mentioned Fermat used a similar approach to determine the tangent line to an arbitrary point on a parabola. Rene Descartes developed a method of finding a normal line to a given point on a curve, from which a tangent line could be determined. He illustrated it with three examples that demonstrated how his method worked; however, in general his method is not easy to apply and does not work for all curves. A third method was developed by Gilles Personne de Roberval which relates the problem of finding a tangent line to a problem of finding the direction of motion at an instant. Ultimately, however, before Newton and Leibniz there was no general method for finding tangent lines, though there were several methods that could work in special cases.
    </p>



    
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Newton">
    <title>Newton and the Development of Calculus</title>

    <p>
      Newton built off the work of Wallis to investigate the area problem. Wallis had found the areas under certain curves such as <m>y=1</m>, <m>y = 1-x^2</m>, <m>y=(1-x^2)^2</m>. To find the area under a more complicated curve such as <m>y = \sqrt{1-x^2}</m>. This is done by noticing that these equations form a pattern <m>(1-x^2)^{0/2}</m>, <m>(1-x^2)^{1/2}</m>, <m>(1-x^2)^{2/2}</m>, <m>(1-x^2)^{3/2}</m>, <m>(1-x^2)^{4/2}</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      It follows from this that the area under the curve <m>(1-x^2)^{1/2}</m> lies between those of <m>y=1</m> and <m>y = 1-x^2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Newton made the observation that the curve <m>y = (1-x^2)^{0/2} = 1</m> sits above an area of <m>x</m>, the curve <m>y = (1-x^2)^{2/2} = 1-x^2</m> sits above an area of <m>x-\frac{1}{3}x^3</m>, and the curve <m>y = (1-x^2)^{4/2} = 1-2x^2+x^4</m> sits above an area of <m>x - \frac{2}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{5}x^5</m>. Newton spotted the pattern and came up with the following rules for finding areas.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The general method which I had devised some time ago for measuring the quantity of curves by an infinite series of terms you have, in the following, rather briefly explained than narrowly demonstrated. To the base <m>AB</m> of some curve <m>AD</m> let the ordinate <m>BD</m> be perpendicular and let <m>AB</m> be called <m>x</m> and <m>BD</m> <m>y</m>. Let <m>a, b, c \ldots</m> be given quantities and <m>m,n</m> integers. Then
      </p>

      <p>
        Rule 1: If <m>ax^{m/n} = y</m>, then will <m>(na/(m+n))x^{(m+n)/n}</m> equal the area <m>ABD</m>. The matter will be evident by example. 
      </p>

      <p>
        Example 1: If <m>x^2=y</m>, that is, if <m>a = n = 1</m> and <m>m=2</m>, then <m>(\frac{1}{3})x^3 = ABD</m>. 
      </p>

      <p>
        Example 2: If <m>4\sqrt{x} = 4x^{1/2} = y</m>, then <m>\frac{8}{3}x^{3/2} = ABD</m>
      </p>

      <p>
        Example 4: If <m>\frac{1}{x^2} = x^{-2}</m>, that is, if <m>a = n = 1</m> and <m>m = -2</m> then <m>((1/-1)x^{-1}) = -x^{-1} = \alpha BD</m> infinitely extended in the direction of <m>\alpha</m>; the computation sets its sign negative because it lies on the further side of the line <m>BD</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Example 5: If <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2}} = y</m>, then <m>(\frac{2}{-1})x^{-1/2} = BD\alpha</m> 
      </p>

      <p>
        Example 6: If <m>(1/x) = y</m> then <m>(\frac{1}{0})x^{0/1} = (\frac{1}{0}) = infinity</m> just as the area of the hyperbola is on each side of the line <m>BD</m>. 
      </p>

    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Essentially Newton's Rule 1 states that the area under <m>y = ax^{m/n}</m> is <m>\frac{na}{n+m}x^{(n+m)/n}</m> provided that <m>\frac{m}{n} \neq -1</m>. As Example 6 shows the rule breaks down in this case. Newton also had a second rule for finding areas of curves that are combinations of curves of the kind that area computed with the first rule such as <m>x^2+x^{3/2}</m>. The area underneath such a curve is the sum of the areas underneath the simpler curves.  
    </p>

    <p>
      From Newton's writings, we know that he was familiar with Rule 1 by 1665. He had also developed a method for finding tangents that was similar to Fermat's adequality method. We will consider Newton's method applied to the curve <m>x^3+y^3 -3axy = 0</m>, which is known as the folium of Descartes. The problem is to find a tangent to this curve.
    </p>

    <p>
      To solve this problem, Newton let's <m>o</m> denote a very small quantity and argues that what is <m>(x,y)</m> at one instant will be <m>(x+p0, y+q0)</m> in the next. Thus
      <md>
        (x+po)^3 + (y+qo)^3 -3a(x+po)(y+qo) = 0.
      </md>
      Expanding the brackets gives that 

      <md>
        x^3+3x^2po + 3x(po)^2 + (po)^3 + y^3+3y^2qo + 3y(qo)^2 + (qo)^3 - 3axy - 3axqo - 3aypo - 3apoqo = 0
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Since the original equation is <m>x^3+y^3-3axy = 0</m>, Newton deduced that the terms with <m>o</m> in it must sum to <m>0</m>. Dividing those terms by <m>o</m> yields the equation
      <md>
        3x^2p + 3xp^2o +p^3o^2 + 3y^2q + 3yq^2o + q^3o^2 - 3axq - 3ayp - 3apqo = 0
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Since <m>o</m> is very small, Newton argued that the terms with <m>o</m> were negligible compared to the ones without <m>o</m>. Dropping those terms leaves
      <md>
        3x^2p + 3y^2q - 3axq - 3ayp = 0
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Finally the slope of the tangent will be <m>\frac{q}{p}</m> which in this case is 

      <md>
        \frac{q}{p} = \frac{x^2-ay}{ax-y^2}. 
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Newton's method for finding tangents works because the slope of the secant line from <m>(x,y)</m> to <m>(x+po, y+qo)</m> is <m>\frac{p}{q}</m>. When <m>o</m> is very small the secant line is approximately a tangent line.
    </p>

    <p>
      Newton also explored the connection between the area problem and the tangent problem. In his writings he considered a curve given by an unknown equation between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. The area underneath it is taken to be <m>z = \frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}</m>. Newton argued that if this is the area then the curve is <m>y = x^{1/2}</m>, which is precisely what you obtain when you apply his <m>o</m>-method to find the tangent through the curve <m>y = \frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}</m>. Newton had realized that the problem of finding the area under a curve is the inverse of the tangent problem. Newton's discovery is the basis of the modern Fundamental Theorem of Calculus; however, Newton viewed his result as more of a definition than a theorem. In Newton's mind if you wanted to find the area underneath a curve, you would look at a table of tangents until you found a tangent with the same equation as your curve. From there you could find the original function, which would give you the area under the curve. For example, if you wanted to find the area of <m>y = x^{1/2}</m>, you would guess that the area under the curve is <m>z = \frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}</m>. From there you can check your guess by finding the slope of the tangent at <m>x</m>, which is <m>y=x^{1/2}</m>. Since this is the equation we started with, it confirms that our guess is correct and that we have found the area under the curve.  
    </p>

    <p>
      Newton's writings show that his method could be used to find areas for a variety of curves, both algebraic and transcendental. The one advantage that Newton's approach had over previous methods was that it allowed more complicated tangents to be broken into sums of simpler tangents. Using modern notation, if Newton found that the tangent of <m>y = f(x)</m> at <m>a</m> was <m>f'(a)</m> and the tangent of <m>y = g(x)</m> at <m>a</m> was <m>g'(a)</m> then his methods would imply that the tangent of <m> y = f(x)+g(x)</m> at <m>a</m> was <m>f'(a)+g'(a)</m>.
    </p>

    
  </subsection>

  </section>
